{"body":"Required outcome: deliver every migrated observability child with its original criteria. This epic stays open until all required children and shared gates complete.\n\n## Ordered implementation backlog\n\nAll criteria below are mandatory completion gates for the applicable implementation slice. They describe future assertions, not tests already implemented or passing. A task is complete only when its criteria are covered by committed automated tests registered with the project test runner/CI, and passing evidence records the commands, configurations and platforms. If a task is split, each split-out item must receive its own registered key and name its covered criteria; the parent remains incomplete until all are satisfied. Manual inspection, log screenshots and the observation-only research runner are insufficient acceptance evidence.\n\nUse unit tests for result/state logic and deterministic fault injection, plus subprocess/filesystem/IPC/layer integration tests for externally visible behavior. Assert returned outcomes, exit statuses, structured fields, actual output contents and handle/application behavior; prose-only assertions are appropriate only where human presentation itself is the requirement. Include successful controls alongside failure cases. Upgrade E1–E9 into assertion-bearing regressions as their owning tasks are implemented. Tests must expose the defect before its fix where feasible, rather than merely mirror implementation internals.\n\nConcurrency/deadline tests must use fake clocks, barriers and controllable adapters instead of random driver instability or fragile sleeps. Give process tests watchdogs and isolated temporary output directories. Linux and Windows process changes require platform-specific automated evidence; GPU-independent mocks are the baseline, with hardware jobs for hardware claims. An unavailable platform/device is skipped/unverified and leaves that acceptance criterion open. Record applicable debug/release and exception-enabled/disabled coverage; use sanitizer jobs where useful for changed parsing, lifetime or concurrency code. Tests must check honest unknown outcomes where evidence is deliberately lost.\n\n**Compatibility gate for every affected public boundary:** declare whether source, binary, protocol and behavioral compatibility are preserved or intentionally changed. Maintain compile/run fixtures for preserved old callers/invocations, schema/protocol fixtures for preserved consumers and prebuilt-consumer checks only where binary compatibility is claimed. Assert and document intentional changes, including formerly false-success CLI exits. Additive signatures alone do not establish compatibility; do not claim that all existing callers continue working. Require structured outcomes without prose parsing once the relevant result API/mode is introduced.\n\n\n## Dependency and delivery notes\n\nThe critical path is **FOS-000005 → FOS-000009 → (FOS-000010 and FOS-000011) → FOS-000012 → FOS-000013 → FOS-000014**: worker facts precede reconciliation, which precedes a trustworthy controller/API result. **FOS-000003 → FOS-000016 → FOS-000017 → FOS-000019** is the persistence/capture path. **FOS-000006** prevents later runs from trusting a failed attempt. **FOS-000004** is an early containment fix, not a substitute for the richer accounting. Initial result schemas may report persistence/support detail as unknown until the respective producers are migrated; they must not imply whole-standard compliance early.\n\nKeep regression verification alongside every iteration. Do not defer all tests to `FOS-000024`. The highest-value fixtures are E1–E9, deterministic worker failure/deadline injection, duplicate/stale messages, full diagnostic rings, close-time ENOSPC, optional versus required output loss, and cancellation before/after final publication. Linux and Windows process implementations need the same behavioral matrix even when mechanisms differ. Apple/Android builds use `NO_ROBUST_REPLAYER`; ordinary replay/library/capture contracts still apply without inventing worker support there.\n\n\n## Remaining verification\n\nBefore implementation is accepted, run supported CMake builds and tests on Linux and Windows, exception-enabled/disabled and release/debug configurations as applicable. Add deterministic fault injection rather than relying solely on the existing random `SIMULATE_UNSTABLE_DRIVER` hooks. Exercise runtime capture with multiple instances/devices and real Vulkan support failures; test cache feedback on hardware that exposes it. No current probe demonstrates actual cache warming or durable driver output.\n\n\n## Unkeyed report disposition\n# Repeated shader processing with unclear failure and cache-reuse outcomes\n\nThe Sims 4 repeatedly delayed startup with “Processing Vulkan shaders,” despite previous processing having finished.\n\nSteam launched Fossilize’s replayer before Proton, with a **10-second limit without pipeline completion**. Controlled tests showed workers timing out and compilation being interrupted. Allowing 60 seconds let the tested workload finish; its resulting cache was reusable.\n\nThe investigation was complicated by several observability gaps:\n\n- **Hidden failure context:** Steam’s normal logs showed progress and completion without an actionable explanation of worker failures. Separate, unsuppressed replay runs exposed the timeouts.\n- **Ambiguous crash reporting:** Timeouts used SIGABRT and the crash-recovery path. A debugger was needed to establish that an interrupted worker was executing inside NVIDIA’s shader compiler; this did not prove a spontaneous driver crash.\n- **Unclear completion:** One replay returned exit status zero despite reporting “Cannot continue,” making successful termination insufficient evidence of completed work.\n- **Unclear reuse:** The logs did not clearly connect replay triggers, previously processed entries, skipped/failed work and persisted results. A shader-bucket update also triggered processing of an unchanged generic database.\n\n**The integration gap:** Fossilize needs to expose explicit failure reasons and partial outcomes even in quiet mode. Steam needs to retain and present those results alongside why replay was scheduled and what reuse information is available.\n\nThe investigation did **not** establish that the entire cache was discarded. Cache reuse worked in controlled tests, while deliberately forcing a warm replay still incurred substantial processing overhead.\n\n## References\n\nInvestigated 26–27 October 2026.\n\n- [Upstream issue: steam-for-linux #13810](https://github.com/ValveSoftware/steam-for-linux/issues/13810)\n- [Sanitized supporting evidence](https://gist.github.com/robinwassen/6d8cd12e4040cf409cb3553f4fb87b61)\n\nFossilize obligations overlap FOS-000004/000005/000007/000009/000012/000013/000014/000022; retained individually without a duplicate issue. Steam scheduling/presentation belongs to external steam-for-linux#13810; no new Fossilize implementation requirement.\n\nSpecifications, reproduced E1..E9 findings, adversarial review and sanitized fixtures are supporting evidence, not additional task definitions.\n\n<!-- fossilize-migration-v1:observability -->\n","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOVZpjbc8AAAABgbfVEQ","author":{"login":"robinwassen"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"## Legacy identity mapping\n\n- `FOS-000001` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/10\n- `FOS-000002` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/11\n- `FOS-000003` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/12\n- `FOS-000004` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/13\n- `FOS-000005` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/14\n- `FOS-000006` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/15\n- `FOS-000007` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/16\n- `FOS-000008` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/17\n- `FOS-000009` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/18\n- `FOS-000010` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/19\n- `FOS-000011` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/20\n- `FOS-000012` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/21\n- `FOS-000013` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/22\n- `FOS-000014` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/23\n- `FOS-000015` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/24\n- `FOS-000016` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/25\n- `FOS-000017` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/26\n- `FOS-000018` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/27\n- `FOS-000019` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/28\n- `FOS-000020` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/29\n- `FOS-000021` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/30\n- `FOS-000022` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/31\n- `FOS-000023` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/32\n- `FOS-000024` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/33\n- `FOS-000025` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/34\n- `FOS-000026` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/35\n- `FOS-000027` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/36\n- `FOS-000028` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/37\n- `harness` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/9\n- `observability` → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8\n\nDependencies are native blocked-by edges. Every declared edge is required, including complete-propagation prerequisites. Numeric source order is sequencing advice; parent membership adds no blocking edge. A cancelled/not-planned prerequisite does not satisfy its obligation.\n\nCritical paths: https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/14 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/18 → (https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/19 and https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/20) → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/21 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/22 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/23; persistence/capture: https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/12 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/25 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/26 → https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/28.\n\n## Dependency reasons (verbatim metadata)\n\n- `FOS-000005`: 5. **FOS-000005 — Separate replay-thread work completion from item success.** **M; depends on `FOS-000001` and `FOS-000004` for complete propagation; §2–§3.** [worker:1800](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1800) discards the parse return and increments `completed_count` after processing; creation helpers return void. [normal replay:4553](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L4553) returns success after the queue finishes (E7). Introduce a thread-safe outcome accumulator independent of optional shared memory. First cover parse/read and Vulkan creation failures, then setup/validation/support/dependency exits. Propagate the result through normal replay, child and controller. **Check:** E7, missing entry, injected Vulkan failure, and mixed success/failure. Keep queue progress as queue progress. Unsupported required work must be unsuccessful under the declared policy, even when it is not a crash.\n- `FOS-000006`: 6. **FOS-000006 — Stop replay-cache markers from certifying failed creation.** **S once `FOS-000005` exists; depends on `FOS-000005`; §2, §6.** [creation dispatch:1698](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1698) calls void creation routines, then calls `mark_replayed_resource()` for a recognized resource type regardless of Vulkan success. [marker:1196](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1196) also ignores its database write result. This is a source-confirmed path, not a reproduced real-driver failure. Write a successful-work marker only after the applicable creation/validation/output contract succeeds. Keep validation-whitelist meaning separate from successful compilation. **Check:** inject creation failure with no validation callback, rerun with the same replayer-cache and require the item to remain eligible. Test `--loop` and compile-required fallback so an eventual success is handled deliberately. Existing marker files lack stronger provenance; do not silently relabel their contents as verified reusable output.\n- `FOS-000007`: 7. **FOS-000007 — Record timeout origin before invoking crash recovery.** **S–M; depends on `FOS-000004`; §4–§5.** [no-progress wait:965](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L965) invokes the timeout handler before its diagnostic. [Linux:1589](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_linux.hpp#L1589) sends SIGABRT; [Windows:1203](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_windows.hpp#L1203) writes `CRASH`. Store/send a distinct trigger from the healthy dispatcher before termination. Distinguish configured work-no-progress timeout, controller heartbeat timeout, and post-crash cleanup timeout. Include actual monotonic elapsed time and configured threshold. **Check:** deterministic no-progress injection, spontaneous fault, heartbeat loss, failed signal delivery and escalation. An externally observed SIGKILL alone does not prove a controller timeout or OOM. Multiple threads imply unknown individual attribution unless independently established.\n- `FOS-000008`: 8. **FOS-000008 — Reject malformed resource envelopes as controlled input errors.** **S for envelope guard, M for nested validation; depends on `FOS-000001` and `FOS-000005`; §1–§2.** [parse:6368](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize.cpp#L6368) indexes `doc[\"version\"].GetInt()` without checking object/member/type (E5). Start with the envelope, reporting invalid shape versus incompatible version; audit nested field reads in subsequent bounded patches. Preserve numeric parse error/offset and resource identity without dumping shader contents. **Check:** `{}`, `[]`, null, missing/string version, malformed nested records; none may assert, terminate the embedding host or be blamed on the driver. Test release and debug builds. This is an outcome/containment prerequisite, not merely prettier logging; no claim of a complete parser-security audit is made here.\n- `FOS-000009`: 9. **FOS-000009 — Carry reason data from the place that detects it.** **M; depends on `FOS-000005`, `FOS-000007`, `FOS-000008`; §1, §4–§5.** Add a small stable internal result/event representation: phase, reason ID, known raw error, tag/hash or range, worker and attempt, plus unknown-context flags. Start with parse, creation and timeout paths. Extend child messages on both platforms with explicit framing/version negotiation and bounded parsing; never require the controller to infer categories from human logs. **Check:** split/truncated messages, unknown version/reason, missing final message, worker exit before first report. Unknown information stays unknown. This is where downstream controllers acquire causes; a parent-only summary cannot recover details a worker never sends.\n- `FOS-000010`: 10. **FOS-000010 — Explain unsupported features and blocked dependencies.** **M, staged by resource type; depends on `FOS-000009`; §1–§2.** Feature filter predicates return bool, often at detailed feature/property checks; [setup:1270](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1270) reduces this to “not supported.” Return feature/extension/property identifiers and relevant limits when known. Pilot compute, then graphics/ray-tracing/static objects. Link missing/failed parents or modules to blocked consumers rather than counting consumers as independent compiler faults. **Check:** one unsupported feature; one invalid module referenced by many pipelines; missing common-cache input; unknown pNext sType. Preserve existing support advice only where justified. Do not make “unsupported” a catch-all for parse, OOM or missing dependency failures.\n- `FOS-000011`: 11. **FOS-000011 — Expose discovery and selection completeness.** **M; depends on `FOS-000009`; §2.** [stream scanning:1200](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_db.cpp#L1200) intentionally salvages truncated tails (E9). Database composites may omit unavailable inputs; whitelists/ranges and replay-cache checks change observable work. Return preparation/discovery metadata: requested/opened inputs, excluded inputs/items with policy, selected logical scope, truncation/read failures, complete/unknown discovery. Keep salvage behavior and report its limits. **Check:** intact empty archive versus truncated archive with zero retained entries; missing optional versus required subdatabase; filters and ranges. Known processed items cannot establish full coverage when unread scope is unknown. A caller may explicitly accept a recovered prefix, but the report must name that scope.\n- `FOS-000012`: 12. **FOS-000012 — Reconcile final logical items across recovery attempts.** **M, split types/range-recovery if necessary; depends on `FOS-000009`, `FOS-000010`, `FOS-000011`; §2–§4.** Current atomics count activity and broad skips, while [condensed progress:71](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_external_replayer.cpp#L71) explicitly clamps/weights counts and documents retry duplication. Preserve that UI estimate, but add authoritative accounting by selected `(tag, hash)` and attempt within a run/input scope. Distinguish selected work from extra dependencies and benchmark repetitions. Each selected item gets one final outcome; attempts retain their history. **Check:** duplicate/reordered messages, stale attempt failure after successful retry, one crash plus skipped culprit plus successful tail, unreported worker work. Do not infer exact successful counts by subtracting overlapping failure counters. Bound memory using range summaries/compact tables where possible; lost evidence must remain unknown.\n- `FOS-000013`: 13. **FOS-000013 — Publish one versioned replay result and derive exits from it.** **M; depends on `FOS-000003`, `FOS-000004`, `FOS-000005`, `FOS-000006`, `FOS-000007`, `FOS-000008`, `FOS-000009`, `FOS-000010`, `FOS-000011`, `FOS-000012`; §2–§5.** Introduce an explicit structured destination independent of verbosity, separate from stdout command payloads. Include declared policy/scope, execution completion, coverage, known/unknown reason counts, recovery and output status. Render the compact human failure summary from the same result. Document aggregate success/failure/invalid-invocation status; `1`/`2` is a reasonable choice, while internal child codes are a separate protocol. **Check:** controlled early failure, mixed categories, clean run, quiet workers, unwritable result destination and truncated output. Write a result with honest unavailable fields before waiting for every subsystem migration. Unknown completion of a required output prevents a success claim; later producer migrations improve the precision of that result. Completion publication must occur after required outputs; Linux currently sets `progress_complete` before identifier merging ([Linux:1383](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_linux.hpp#L1383)). Do not use the result mode to promise GPU cache durability.\n- `FOS-000014`: 14. **FOS-000014 — Retain authoritative results independently of lossy progress events.** **M; depends on `FOS-000012`, `FOS-000013`; §4–§5.** [control block](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_external_replayer_control_block.hpp#L29) explicitly permits drops; controller calls commonly ignore `shared_control_block_write()` failure. Preserve a final snapshot/result outside that informative ring, expose dropped-event counts, and add a result query to `ExternalReplayer`. Keep `wait()` process status and `poll_progress()` activity semantics distinguishable from the operation outcome. Version cookie/layout and producer/consumer compatibility need an explicit migration; do not append fields without auditing mapped-size assumptions. **Check:** saturated ring, no polling until exit, process death before final publication, delayed old attempt, old/new protocol mismatch. E6 must be diagnosable through the API with stdout/stderr discarded.\n- `FOS-000015`: 15. **FOS-000015 — Report cancellation and bound orderly shutdown reporting.** **M; depends on `FOS-000004`, `FOS-000009`, `FOS-000013`, `FOS-000014`; §3–§4.** Linux SIGINT/SIGTERM and sentinel closure call `shutdown_processes()` ([Linux:896](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_linux.hpp#L896), [Linux:1335](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_linux.hpp#L1335)); expected kills suppress crash treatment but do not establish completed work. Retain cancellation origin, unfinished scope and preserved outputs; preserve platform CLI termination conventions. From healthy control flow attempt the final result with bounded cleanup. Avoid adding allocation/locking/stdio serialization to fatal signal handlers: reuse minimal preallocated evidence and let the controller report. **Check:** user cancellation, parent/sentinel loss, forced kill during cleanup, blocked result channel. A missing final result is unknown, not successful. No final-report guarantee after SIGKILL.\n- `FOS-000016`: 16. **FOS-000016 — Add database finalization that can report failure.** **M, one backend at a time; depends on `FOS-000003` and `FOS-000009`; §1, §6.** [DatabaseInterface:149](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_db.hpp#L149) exposes void flush; [StreamArchive:1072](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_db.cpp#L1072) ignores flush/close failures; [ZIP:776](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_db.cpp#L776) only logs finalization failures during destruction. Provide explicit checked finalization before nonthrowing destruction and sticky I/O status with underlying error. Start with stream archives (E4), then directory/ZIP/concurrent wrappers. Preserve valid prefixes and define behavior after a partial entry write; do not blindly retry into a damaged tail. **Check:** buffered ENOSPC, partial write, ZIP finalize failure, child-backend failure, repeated finalize. Audit public virtual-interface compatibility: use a deliberate additive/versioned migration rather than casually breaking external implementations. Promise flush/close completion unless stronger durability is explicitly implemented.\n- `FOS-000017`: 17. **FOS-000017 — Expose recorder background completion and recording loss.** **M; depends on `FOS-000009` and `FOS-000016`; §1, §6.** [prepare:9117](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize.cpp#L9117) clears the database pointer on failure; writes throughout `record_task()` ignore bool results; [teardown:12530](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize.cpp#L12530) returns void. Add a queryable sticky recording outcome and an explicit completion operation. Distinguish accepted/queued, serialized, written and finalized; specify ownership so the recorder flushes borrowed databases while their owner performs close/finalization. Account separately for primary, identifier, last-use and pipeline-use outputs and intentional application filtering. **Check:** E1/E2, late close failure, failed hash/dependency resolution after acceptance, synchronized mode, multiple recorder sessions. Status must work with `LOG_NONE` and no callback; keep application Vulkan behavior unchanged.\n- `FOS-000018`: 18. **FOS-000018 — Expose typed library operation reasons beyond the recorder.** **M, incremental entry points; depends on `FOS-000008`, `FOS-000009`, `FOS-000010`, `FOS-000011`, `FOS-000016`, `FOS-000017`; §1.** Public parse/record/database methods generally return bool, conflating unsupported state, corrupt input, missing dependency and I/O failure. Add result-bearing APIs while preserving compatibility wrappers where feasible. First cover `StateReplayer::parse` and recording rejection, then application-filter/database prepare/read/write. Include affected resource and known underlying error, not a mandatory prose parser. **Check:** same false result from incompatible version, unknown sType, missing referenced resource and write failure now produces distinct caller-actionable reasons. Callback absence must not erase these results. Queued `StateCreatorInterface` acceptance and eventual host-created object completion require an explicit contract; Fossilize cannot infer arbitrary host async success.\n- `FOS-000019`: 19. **FOS-000019 — Publish capture completeness without changing application results.** **M; depends on `FOS-000017`, `FOS-000018`; §1–§2, §6.** [layer dispatch:1203](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/layer/dispatch.cpp#L1203) already preserves driver results across ordinary recording failures; build on that. [instance:509](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/layer/instance.cpp#L509) creates/configures the recorder but has no persistent capture-outcome interface. Add per-recorder/application scope status, explicit filtered/disabled state, missing resources and finalization outcome, then a documented query/report mechanism owned by the layer/integrator. **Check:** Vulkan success plus capture failure, capture unsupported feature, failed prepare, concurrent instances/devices and teardown. Do not conflate application compilation failure with recording loss. The opt-in emergency-dump modes intentionally alter signal handling; retain and disclose that mode rather than claiming every current mode guarantees host continuity.\n- `FOS-000020`: 20. **FOS-000020 — Make library presentation optional and define callback safety.** **M; depends on `FOS-000017`, `FOS-000018`, `FOS-000019` for log-independent truth; §1, §5.** A callback and inherited recorder-thread configuration already exist in [errors:42](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_errors.hpp#L42), with tests. [callback:12666](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize.cpp#L12666) calls the sink synchronously with borrowed stack text; direct LOG calls in database bucket setup/application filtering bypass this route. Document thread/lifetime/reentrancy rules; route recoverable library presentation through the optional host sink and audit direct stderr fallbacks. Avoid invoking user callbacks under internal locks; provide recursion protection and explicit truncation/drop accounting. Exception-enabled builds need containment; default builds disable exceptions. **Check:** no sink/no unsolicited streams, reentrant callback, throwing callback where enabled, expired userdata prevention by contract, long messages. A timeout cannot safely preempt an arbitrary C++ callback: use an explicit nonblocking sink contract or a bounded queued delivery mode, not an impossible guarantee.\n- `FOS-000021`: 21. **FOS-000021 — Propagate required-output failures and preserve merge evidence.** **M, one output family per change; depends on `FOS-000003`, `FOS-000013`, `FOS-000016`, `FOS-000017`; §2, §6.** [pipeline-cache export:1835](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1835) loses retrieval/write/close outcomes; [stats CSV:4053](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L4053) silently returns on open failure; `dump_stats()` has no returned outcome. Identifier merging logs failure and then removes child archives ([Linux:1417](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay_linux.hpp#L1417), analogous Windows). Define which requested outputs are required and make their failure affect the final result. Remove source artifacts only after checked successful merge/publication; retain recoverable parts otherwise. **Check:** missing directory, ENOSPC at close, Vulkan cache-data retrieval failure, one malformed stats shard, merge failure. No success publication before required exports finish. Optional telemetry failure remains distinct from required output failure.\n- `FOS-000022`: 22. **FOS-000022 — Describe reuse evidence and compatibility honestly.** **M; depends on `FOS-000006`, `FOS-000011`, `FOS-000012`, `FOS-000013`, `FOS-000014`, `FOS-000021`; §5–§6.** Report separate facts for replayer-cache marker skips ([replay:3144](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L3144)), application pipeline-cache feedback ([replay:1479](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1479)), shader identifiers and opaque driver-managed caches. Include build/tool version, input identities, effective filters/options, null-device mode, device/driver/API and relevant UUID context, deadlines and duration units. Existing GPU and timing logs are useful but not a retained structured context record. **Check:** unavailable feedback stays unknown, simulated null-device run cannot claim GPU warming, legacy markers do not prove persistence, changed device/driver context is visible. Metadata alone is not proof of cache hits. Steam's scheduling rationale remains outside Fossilize's evidence.\n- `FOS-000023`: 23. **FOS-000023 — Migrate remaining CLI contracts and internal consumers.** **M, separate small tool changes; depends on `FOS-000002`, `FOS-000013`, `FOS-000016`, `FOS-000017`, `FOS-000018`, `FOS-000021`, `FOS-000022`; §2–§6.** Extend result/exit/output conventions to convert/merge, opt/disasm, prune/rehash, synth, bench, feature-sifter and cache-roundtrip validation. Conversion and merge already check many immediate bool failures; add missing phase/resource details and checked finalization, not a wholesale rewrite. [roundtrip:230](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_validate_cache_roundtrip.cpp#L230) parses console prose for hits/misses; consume versioned results instead. Explicitly reject mixed misses when the roundtrip policy requires all hits (current branch only rejects zero hits plus nonzero misses). [bench:296](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_bench.cpp#L296) returns `EXIT_FAILURE` from a bool function, converting failure to true. **Check:** each tool's success, invalid invocation, partial/unsupported work and output-delivery failure; optimizer fallback policy and intentional pruning remain explicit. Keep list payloads on stdout and reports separate. Structured results must cover controlled failures too, not only the successful end path.\n- `FOS-000024`: 24. **FOS-000024 — Bound diagnostics without sampling away outcomes.** **M, subsystem increments; depends on `FOS-000009`, `FOS-000012`, `FOS-000013`, `FOS-000014`, `FOS-000020`; §4–§5.** [spam limiting:88](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/fossilize_errors.hpp#L88) already bounds several call sites, but static counters span calls/recorders and only expose a silencing message; many CLI failures log per item. Add per-run/session reason totals, bounded examples, sampled/dropped/truncated counts and event severity/time. Keep these independent of authoritative final item accounting. Make expensive timing/tracing opt-in and avoid baseline shader dumps, environment values or sensitive absolute paths; retain a caller-controlled path/detail policy. **Check:** many repeated failures, multiple runs in one process, ring overload, long messages and blocked/failed sink. Require bounded baseline memory/overhead and the same final counts with verbose logging enabled or disabled. Establish measurements before picking performance budgets.\n- `FOS-000026`: **Depends on:** FOS-000025 (implementation merged in [PR #2](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/pull/2)).\n- `FOS-000027`: **Depends on:** FOS-000025 (existing harness checkpoint and publication mechanics).\n- `FOS-000028`: **Depends on:** FOS-000025, FOS-000026 and FOS-000027 (merged harness foundations;\n\n<!-- fossilize-migration-v1:identity-map -->\n","createdAt":"2026-10-28T14:11:24Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8#issuecomment-6471275793","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOVZpjbc8AAAABgboufQ","author":{"login":"robinwassen"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"## Historical supporting evidence and disposition\n\nThe [immutable observability research and E1–E9 table](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/73f891d6c0cf498ac92e804d7d0f0cd344a083f5/local-docs/reviews/observability-sweep.md#reproduced-failures) and [sanitized reproduction fixtures](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/tree/73f891d6c0cf498ac92e804d7d0f0cd344a083f5/local-docs/reviews/observability-sweep-evidence) describe historical observations, not passing conformance tests.\n\n- E1/E2: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/26.\n- E3: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/12.\n- E4: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/25.\n- E5: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/13, https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/17.\n- E6: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/13, https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/23.\n- E7: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/14.\n- E8: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/11.\n- E9: original regression obligations are preserved in https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/20.\n\nOriginal allocation/Markdown instructions remain verbatim as historical requirement context. https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/37 explicitly supersedes the tracked-registry rule: future persistent identities come from GitHub; split deliverables still need unique identities and explicit covered criteria. Historical journal high-water25 and committed registry26–28 are retained distinctly as provenance, without inventing earlier reservations.\n\nFossilize obligations overlap FOS-000004/000005/000007/000009/000012/000013/000014/000022; retained individually without a duplicate issue. Steam scheduling/presentation belongs to external steam-for-linux#13810; no new Fossilize implementation requirement.\n\n<!-- fossilize-migration-v1:research-evidence-links -->\n","createdAt":"2026-10-28T14:17:52Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8#issuecomment-6471429757","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":8,"state":"OPEN","title":"Observability: preserve trustworthy outcomes across Fossilize","url":"https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8"}

